// A tooth in its socket, sectioned along its long axis: the standard dental
// finite-element model. Enamel (mat 1) caps a two-cusped crown and thins to
// nothing at the cemento-enamel junction on either side; dentin (mat 2) is
// the body and the root; the pulp (mat 3) is a chamber with a horn under each
// cusp and a canal that narrows down the root and closes above the apex; the
// periodontal ligament (mat 4), a band 0.065 thick, suspends the root in the
// alveolar bone (mat 5) from the bone crest down round the apex. The crown
// and the neck of the root stand free above the bone.
//
// What this domain is for. Smooth, organic, nested interfaces with nothing
// straight or circular in them, carrying three thin features of three
// different kinds:
//
//  * The enamel is a cap whose thickness runs from 0.09 down to zero, so it
//    ends at each junction in a 19-degree wedge on dS: a pinch-out on a
//    curved boundary, where basin's are on straight ones.
//  * The ligament is a U-shaped band of uniform thickness, 2.8 elements at
//    NP = 100 (h = 0.0235 here), wrapped round a convex apex and ending on
//    dS at both crest points.
//  * The pulp canal is 2.9 to 5.4 elements wide and ten times as long: a
//    slender inclusion whose singularities have to fit inside it.
//
// The dentin is a region with one hole (the pulp) and a boundary that is
// part free surface, part enamel and part ligament. The crown is
// asymmetric -- the second cusp is lower -- so nothing is pinned by symmetry,
// and nothing is a full circle.
//
// The outlines are centripetal Catmull-Rom curves through hand-placed control
// points. The enamel's inner face is the crown offset inward by
// 0.09 tanh(s / 0.25) tanh((S - s) / 0.25), with s the arclength from one
// junction and S the crown's length; the ligament's outer face is the root
// offset outward by 0.065 and cut at the crest. All are written as Splines
// through samples 0.01 to 0.015 apart.

// ---- generated geometry ----
